;;; `_` in a guard reads nothing, in silence — regression
;;; `wild-in-guard` (`corpus/regressions.toml`); a bare `_` is
;;; reserved and refused (Q-M1e2.22).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(p a)`, and three rules whose guard writes the wildcard `_`
;;; as an argument of `eq` or `neq`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A guard compares two values, and `_` names none. A bare `_` is reserved
;;; and refused wherever it is written (Q-M1e2.22): the program is refused at
;;; the parse, at the first `_`, and every command that reads it exits 1
;;; (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `32736ac8`, release and debug (every mode and `ein oracle` the same),
;;; the program loaded, and each guard compared `_` as a raw IR node, which
;;; is equal to no value and to another `_`: `(eq ?x _)` never held,
;;; `(neq ?x _)` always did, and `(eq _ _)` held. Root derived `(hit-neq a)`
;;; and `(hit-both a)` and no `hit-eq`, exit 0, with no word of the wildcard
;;; (`ein saturate --dump FILE`, `ein solve FILE`). Today the parse refuses
;;; the `_`: *unexpected `_`: _ (reserved)*.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node) (relation hit-eq Node) (relation hit-neq Node) (relation hit-both Node)
(p a)
(rule e () :match (and (p ?x) (eq ?x _)) :assert (hit-eq ?x))
(rule n () :match (and (p ?x) (neq ?x _)) :assert (hit-neq ?x))
(rule b () :match (and (p ?x) (eq _ _)) :assert (hit-both ?x))
